diff --git a/CHANGELOG.md b/CHANGELOG.md
index 3297453..6dd204a 100644
--- a/CHANGELOG.md
+++ b/CHANGELOG.md
@@ -111,6 +111,9 @@
   - `build_key()` (used internally by `ThrottleBackend.get_key(...)` when args/kwargs are passed) now hashes with FNV-1a 64-bit instead of MD5, producing a shorter 16-character digest. Falls back to MD5, not a pure-Python hash, if the compiled extension isn't available, so an uncompiled install doesn't pay a heavy hashing cost.
   - `InMemoryBackend` and `MultiProcessInMemoryBackend` now reclaim expired entries via bounded random sampling (Redis-style active expiration) instead of a full scan of every live key/slot on each cleanup pass. Cost per cleanup cycle now scales with a small, configurable sample size, not with the number of live keys - the old `InMemoryBackend._cleanup()` held its shard lock for a full synchronous scan with no `await` inside, which could stall the entire event loop on a shard with many keys. Both backends gain a new `cleanup_sample_size` constructor param (default `20`) controlling how many keys/buckets are checked per shard, per round.
   - `InMemoryBackend.increment()`/`increment_with_ttl()` no longer round-trip counter values through `str()` internally; they store the raw `int` and only stringify at the `get()`/`multi_get()` read boundary, preserving the existing `Optional[str]` return contract. `InMemoryBackend`'s shards switched from `OrderedDict` to plain `dict` - insertion order was never actually relied on (no LRU eviction, no `max_keys`).
+  - Added `traffik._hashing.fnv_64bit_hash_hex()`: same hash as `fnv_64bit_hash()`, written directly as a 16-character hex string in C. `build_key()` now uses it instead of `format(fnv_64bit_hash(data), "016x")` - measured ~2x faster, since that Python-level `format()` call cost about as much as the hash itself.
+  - Added `traffik.config.get_legacy_md5_keys()`/`set_legacy_md5_keys()` (env var `TRAFFIK_LEGACY_MD5_KEYS`): forces `build_key()` back to MD5. Resolved once at import time (like the compiled-extension-availability check itself), not per call, so it adds no overhead when unused. Set before `traffik.backends.base` is first imported to take effect.
+  - Renamed `_cext` to `_extensions` (and `_atomic.c` to `atomic.c`, for consistency with `hashing.c`) - source layout only, doesn't change any importable module name.
 
 - **Packaging**:
   - The `.pyi` stub files for the C extensions are now actually included in built wheels (`package_data` previously only covered `py.typed` under the top-level `traffik` package, so `_ext.pyi` under `traffik.backends` was silently never shipped).
